--TEST--
`Core\Session::start` on the `shared` backend reaches the one store `[cache.shared]` names, and says so when there is none -- `rule:http-server/a-session-store-answers-four-operations` and `rule:http-server/session-backend-is-shared-or-db-and-local-is-refused-at-boot`, `rule:concurrency/the-local-tier-cannot-hold-what-must-be-coherent`
--GET--
under=a-request
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The case answers a request, because `start` asks whether there is one before
// it asks anything about the store
// (`rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`), so the store question
// below is out of reach without one.
//
// The tree below chooses a store — so the refusal of the neighbouring case, the
// one that names `[session] backend`, cannot be what arrives here. What arrives
// instead names `[cache.shared] url`, and that is the assertion: `rule:http-server/session-backend-is-shared-or-db-and-local-is-refused-at-boot`
// says a deployment has *one* shared store, and a session store that named a
// second address would be a second thing to configure for no coherence the
// first does not already have. A member that had opened an address of its own
// would have got past this line.
uint $named = 0;
string $said = "";
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (RuntimeError $nowhere) {
    $said = $nowhere->message;
    if (Core\Str::contains($said, "[cache.shared] url")) {
        $named = $named + 1;
    }
}
echo "the shared backend asks `[cache.shared]` for its store ", $named, " of 1\n";

// And it got there without naming the block that *is* written, which is the
// other half: a member that reported both would be reporting the first question
// it asked rather than the one it failed.
if (Core\Str::contains($said, "[session] backend")) {
    echo "but it still complained about the block that is written\n";
} else {
    echo "and the block that is written is not what it complained about\n";
}

// The refusal is the store's absence and not the record's: `rule:concurrency/the-local-tier-cannot-hold-what-must-be-coherent` refuses
// `Core\Session` the per-core tier, so there is no local store for this to have
// quietly fallen back to, and a fallback is what a second call would reveal.
uint $again = 0;
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (RuntimeError $still) {
    $again = $again + 1;
}
echo "and it refuses every time, ", $again, " of 1\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A store § 3 admits, chosen and then not addressed — which is a real
# deployment's first five minutes, and the one shape that tells the two
# refusals apart.
[session]
backend = "shared"
--EXPECT--
the shared backend asks `[cache.shared]` for its store 1 of 1
and the block that is written is not what it complained about
and it refuses every time, 1 of 1
